# Every .py in this repository is LF in the index AND in the working tree.
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise check them out as CRLF, and the harnesses
# assert LF on the working-tree copy, not on the committed blob. Measured on
# 2026-10-11: harness 32 A03 ("LF only backend/embedded.py") flipped to FAIL
# after an edit rewrote that file through a CRLF-converting checkout, while the
# committed blob was still pure LF (crlf 0 / lf 280). Declaring the text
# attribute here makes the checkout filter LF for both sides, so the assertion
# and the index agree on one newline. The rules below are matched later and
# therefore still win for the vendored paths.
*.py text eol=lf

# V41-A03 (2026-10-21): the three non-.py text files the harnesses read. The
# `*.py` rule above does not reach them, so they sat outside any newline rule.
# Measured with `git check-attr text eol` on 2026-10-19: metadata.txt,
# resources/style.qss and CHANGELOG.md all reported "unspecified" while
# ui/dock.py reported "text: set / eol: lf". The index holds LF for all three;
# the working tree held CRLF for CHANGELOG.md (`git ls-files --eol`: i/lf
# w/crlf), which is what made `git diff` warn "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# and would have let a fresh checkout plus a rebuild produce bytes that differ
# from the recorded release zip. Declaring the attribute pins one newline on
# both sides, for the same reason as the `*.py` rule above.
#
# This declaration packages no new bytes. metadata.txt and resources/style.qss
# ship inside the release zip and were already LF in the working tree
# (measured 2026-10-21: every zip from 0.1.0 to 0.5.4 carries both members
# with crlf=0). CHANGELOG.md is not packaged at all - harness 17 L2 asserts it
# is absent from the zip - and its working-tree copy was renormalized to LF in
# this same change, so the tree now matches what a fresh checkout produces.
upwise_chat_for_qgis/metadata.txt text eol=lf
upwise_chat_for_qgis/resources/style.qss text eol=lf
CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf

# This repository is configured with core.autocrlf=true, so a fresh clone
# rewrites LF to CRLF in the working tree. For the vendored upstream sources
# that silently breaks two things we assert elsewhere:
#
#   * mcpserver/ is supposed to be byte-identical to qgis_mcp_plugin 0.15.0,
#     so that diffing a newer upstream release against it is mechanical.
#   * mcpserver/VENDOR.md records a sha256 per file. Under CRLF conversion a
#     clone computes different digests than the machine that wrote them, and
#     the integrity check fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the
#     files' contents.
#
# Measured on 2026-10-11: a clone of this branch produced wire.py at 7571
# bytes (CRLF) where upstream is 7371 bytes (LF). `-text` turns newline
# conversion off for these paths; they stay LF everywhere.
upwise_chat_for_qgis/mcpserver/** -text

# Generated asset with its own recorded sha256, same reasoning.
upwise_chat_for_qgis/qgis_tools.json -text

# Collect catalogs (2026-10-21): packaged inside the release zip, so a CRLF
# checkout would make a rebuild differ from the recorded zip bytes - the same
# failure mode as metadata.txt above. vworld is regenerated by scripts/54 and
# datago is hand-maintained; both sat outside every rule (`git check-attr`:
# unspecified) and both are pure LF in index and working tree (measured
# 2026-10-21: crlf=0, lone cr=0). `-text` pins them byte-exact like
# qgis_tools.json.
upwise_chat_for_qgis/collect_catalog_vworld.json -text
upwise_chat_for_qgis/collect_catalog_datago.json -text

# Recorded-session fixtures (2026-10-21): evidence assets whose bytes the
# replay harnesses hash and report. The files carry MIXED newlines on purpose
# (a14/a20/SYNTHETIC_perm_denied are CRLF, s2_live/SYNTHETIC_long are LF), so
# any eol normalization - including autocrlf on a fresh public checkout -
# would silently rewrite two of them and break md5 reproducibility. `-text`
# keeps every byte exactly as recorded.
tests/fixtures/*.jsonl -text
